WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
BOTTLED WATER:
In 2016, bottled water outsold soda for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:10.260
first time in US history. From one perspective,&nbsp;
this is a great thing, people are choosing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:16.680
drink water instead of soda. But, from another&nbsp;
perspective, it’s braindead that Americans are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:23.220
spending so much money on bottled water. It’s one&nbsp;
thing if you don’t have access to clean drinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:30.720
water but this only applies to 0.6% of Americans,&nbsp;
most of whom live in rural areas. Despite this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:37.560
96% of Americans continue to buy bottled water&nbsp;
resulting in the average American consuming 47&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:42.960
gallons of bottled water every year. And much&nbsp;
of this consumption isn’t on hiking trips or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:49.260
vacations. No, Americans literally buy cases of&nbsp;
bottled water and drink it on a regular basis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:58.740
instead of tap water. Families who do this spend&nbsp;
on average wait for it $1,350 per year on bottled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:04.440
water. If you ask them why, they’ll point to the&nbsp;
taste or the filtration or the mineral content,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:10.860
but there’s no evidence to back this up. In&nbsp;
fact, a 2010 study found that the majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:16.260
of participants couldn’t even distinguish&nbsp;
between bottled water and tap water. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:23.340
bottled water is not healthier than tap water. If&nbsp;
anything bottled water is actually worse because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:29.220
93% of bottled water tends to be contaminated&nbsp;
by microplastics. And speaking of plastics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:36.000
86% of bottled water gets thrown away resulting&nbsp;
in nearly 4 billion pounds of plastic trash and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.600
2.5 million tons of carbon dioxide every single&nbsp;
year. So, objectively speaking, bottled water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:49.680
provides no health benefit for 99.4% of Americans&nbsp;
and its expensive financially and environmentally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:57.960
But then, why do people still buy bottled water?&nbsp;
Well, the answer is brilliant marketing. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:03.060
the soda industry isn’t crying over the fact&nbsp;
that people are moving away from soda because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:08.400
they were the ones who engineered this transition&nbsp;
in the first place. Take a look at this lineup of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:14.040
bottled water. At first glance, it looks like a&nbsp;
good assortment of different bottled water. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:20.160
many of these brands are owned and/or distributed&nbsp;
by none other than Coca Cola and Pepsi. Coca Cola&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.860
is involved with Dasani, Ciel, and Smartwater&nbsp;
while Pepsi is involved with Aquafina, Schweppes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:32.220
Life WTR, SoBe, Sparkling Oh, and Evian. And if&nbsp;
a bottled water brand isn’t owned by these two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:39.000
it’s probably owned by Nestle who owns 67 water&nbsp;
brands around the world. So, here’s how these 3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:44.220
behemoths brainwashed America into paying $16&nbsp;
billion for bottled water every single year.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:48.060
A MIRACLE PRODUCT:&nbsp;

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:54.420
Taking a look back, bottled water is by no means&nbsp;
a new invention. The first bottled water plant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.420 --> 00:03:00.420
can be traced back to 1622 to the United Kingdom.&nbsp;
From the very beginning, the premise of bottled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:06.360
water wasn’t exactly scientifically sound. I&nbsp;
mean, the first bottling plant in the UK was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:12.300
literally called “The Holy Well Bottling Plant”.&nbsp;
As you may have guessed, the water promised to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:17.580
offer medicinal value especially when it came&nbsp;
to healing and rejuvenation. Despite its shaky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:22.680
scientific roots, this idea quickly spread across&nbsp;
Europe and we saw bottling plants popping up next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:27.660
to every natural spring across the continent.&nbsp;
This trend was simply accelerated by European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:33.180
colonization and imperialism. As these super&nbsp;
powers traveled to new areas, they were exposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:38.460
to nature that was completely untampered with,&nbsp;
and this simply got them more obsessed with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:43.740
idea of getting their hands on the most natural&nbsp;
water possible. But while this idea was quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:49.380
spreading across Europe, it took quite some time&nbsp;
for it to catch on in America. In fact, the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:55.680
commercially produced bottled water in America&nbsp;
didn’t come about till 1767 or nearly a 150 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:02.160
after the UK. Americans also didn’t buy bottled&nbsp;
water for some sort of miracle reason. Rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:06.660
they were just trying to get access to the&nbsp;
cleanest drinking water they could which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:11.460
actually a serious concern. Back in Europe,&nbsp;
however, bottling plants were simply leaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:17.820
more and more into marketing aspect. In 1783&nbsp;
for example, a Swiss clockmaker named Johann&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:23.100
Jacob Schweppe would discover a way to carbonate&nbsp;
water. Suddenly, bottling plants had another way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:30.600
to market water. Not only was their water holy but&nbsp;
now it was holy and sparkling. Surprisingly, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:36.900
also took quite some time to catch on in America.&nbsp;
It wasn’t till 1809 or 26 years later that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:42.180
first patent for carbonated water was filed in&nbsp;
America. It seems that Americans were surprisingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:46.800
grounded when it came to bottled water. Gimmicks&nbsp;
like holy water and sparkling water didn’t really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:53.580
push them to buy bottled water but disease did&nbsp;
as was the case in the 1850s. Typhoid and cholera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:58.980
outbreaks were becoming increasingly common which&nbsp;
made bottled water highly appealing. And given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:04.500
that the supply of bottled water was somewhat&nbsp;
limited, pricing went through the rought. A single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:11.520
pint of water cost as much as $1.75. Adjusting for&nbsp;
inflation, we’re looking $63 for a pint of water.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:17.760
This was the first time that bottled water started&nbsp;
carrying a certain prestige in America. Only the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:23.100
rich were able to spend $63 on bottled water so&nbsp;
bottled water naturally started being associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:30.480
with a high social status. This was the case for&nbsp;
quite some time as in the next 50 years but in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:36.360
1905 the entire industry would be disrupted. This&nbsp;
year, English doctors would convince authorities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:42.060
to implement public chlorination plants. A&nbsp;
few years later in 1908, New Jersey would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:47.760
implement water chlorination and this marked the&nbsp;
end of bottled water atleast for quite some time.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:54.540
THE TAP TAKES HOLD:
The impact of water chlorination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.540 --> 00:06:00.000
cannot be understated. Water chlorination is&nbsp;
considered to be one of the 10 greatest public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.920
health achievements of the 20th century, and&nbsp;
looking at the numbers, it’s not surprising why.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:11.400
Water chlorination destroyed diseases like Cholera&nbsp;
and Typhoid well before vaccines were developed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:18.540
In 1900, there were 100 cases of Typhoid fever&nbsp;
per 100,000 people. By 1920, this number dropped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:26.700
to 33.8 cases per 100,000 people. And by 2006,&nbsp;
this number dropped to just 0.1 cases per 100,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:32.520
people. And even amongst these cases, 75% of&nbsp;
them occurred amongst international travelers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:38.760
Not only was chlorination extremely effective&nbsp;
but it was also extraordinarily cheap. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:44.040
you can treat a million gallons of water with&nbsp;
just a single dollar. This made it easy for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:48.180
municipalities across the US to implement&nbsp;
water chlorination and within no time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:53.340
most Americans had access to clean drinking water&nbsp;
from their taps. This quickly put an end to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:58.380
popularity of bottled water. The reality was&nbsp;
that bottled water was simply too expensive for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:03.600
the average American. And the only reason that it&nbsp;
was so popular was because drinking tap water was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:09.120
an actual health risk. But now that tap water was&nbsp;
safe to drink, people were quick to forget about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:13.440
bottled water. This isn’t to say that bottling&nbsp;
plants quit trying to sell bottled water though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:19.200
From their perspective, the biggest obstacle was&nbsp;
price. If they could just make bottled water cheap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:24.300
enough, maybe they could convince people to buy&nbsp;
once again. The biggest factor making bottled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:29.760
water so expensive wasn’t actually the water&nbsp;
itself, it was actually the bottling. Water was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:35.280
traditionally bottled in glass, so no wonder&nbsp;
it was expensive. This was only made worse by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:40.260
the great depression as the average person had&nbsp;
less money than ever. This was really the rock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:46.860
bottom for the water bottle industry but things&nbsp;
did slowly get better. In the 1940s, bottlers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:51.960
would start experimenting with plastic bottles.&nbsp;
These were indeed cheaper than glass bottles but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:58.020
only marginally so. It wasn’t until the 1950s and&nbsp;
the introduction of high density polyethylene that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:03.480
bottlers were really able to bring down the price,&nbsp;
but this didn’t help sales like the expected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:08.880
While bottled water was indeed affordable, it&nbsp;
was still significantly more expensive than tap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:13.680
water which made it kind of a stupid purchase&nbsp;
especially when there was no objective benefit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:19.800
This is very much the case to this day. In fact,&nbsp;
bottled water is one of the most highly marked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:25.200
up items in the world. Producing bottled&nbsp;
water only costs 5 cents on average. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:31.080
if you’re paying a $1 for it, you’re looking&nbsp;
at a 2000% markup. For perspective, even movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:37.320
theather popcorn is only marked up 1300%. This&nbsp;
is because somewhere along the line, bottlers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:42.540
finally realized that they simply couldn’t compete&nbsp;
against tap water in terms of price. Tap water was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:47.340
simply always gonna be cheaper. And if bottlers&nbsp;
couldn’t convince customers based on price,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:52.920
they had to convince them based on perceived&nbsp;
quality and that’s exactly what they would do.

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:59.700
TIME TO BRAINWASH:
The extraordinary resurgence of bottled water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:05.760
can be traced back to a single marketing campaign&nbsp;
from a company called Perrier. Perrier was already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:10.620
doing quite well back in France. They were very&nbsp;
much the exception when it came to bottled water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:17.040
as they were not only doing well but they were&nbsp;
exploding in popularity. In fact, between 1946&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:24.180
and 1952, Perrier was able to grow their sales 15X&nbsp;
from 10 million bottles per year to 150 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:28.980
per year. What made their popularity even more&nbsp;
perplexing was that they were seemingly doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:34.380
everything wrong. They had stuck to glass bottles&nbsp;
and they were unapologetically expensive at over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:41.460
$2 per bottle, but the truth was that this was&nbsp;
their secret. Perrier marketed themselves as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:48.300
premium water meant for premium customers. And in&nbsp;
the 1977, Perrier would bring this same marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:54.060
strategy to America. They would spend $5 million&nbsp;
advertising Perrier as Earth’s first soft drink.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.060 --> 00:10:00.120
They claimed that Perrier was more quenching, more&nbsp;
refreshing, and most importantly more natural.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:06.300
This tapped into our natural desire to live longer&nbsp;
and healthier and find the oh so elusive fountain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:11.820
of youth. Perrier would pair this marketing&nbsp;
with brilliant positioning. They would place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:15.480
their water in the most elite restaurants&nbsp;
and hotels in New York and Los Angeles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:20.160
further solidifying the idea that the rich&nbsp;
were living longer and better due to the water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:25.860
they were drinking. This completely redefined&nbsp;
how Americans perceived bottled water. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:30.840
started to believe that bottled water was indeed&nbsp;
better than tap water but it wasn’t quite enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:34.980
to get the average joe to switch over. You see,&nbsp;
Americans were quite busy and arguably addicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:41.400
to drinking something else: soda. But, over time,&nbsp;
awareness about just how unhealthy soda really was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:47.640
started to spread and people would start cutting&nbsp;
back in the late 1990s. Pepsi and Coca Cola were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:53.280
witnessing this stagnation first hand as they saw&nbsp;
their empires slowly melt away. But, that didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:59.580
last for long as both of these guys would swiftly&nbsp;
pivot to water. These guys already had decades of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:04.980
experience advertising sugar water. All they had&nbsp;
to do now was market the water without the sugar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:11.460
Perrier had already created a premium image around&nbsp;
bottled water so all Pepsi and Coca Cola had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:17.760
do was double down. Pepsi would launch Aquafina&nbsp;
and Coca Cola would launch Dasani and they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:23.100
both strategically price bottled water to be&nbsp;
more expensive than soda. Why was water more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:28.680
expensive than soda? Well, obviously because it&nbsp;
was healthier. These two also had connections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:34.320
with basically every retailer on the planet, so&nbsp;
getting their miracle water into stores was no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:39.120
problem at all. And once their product was on&nbsp;
shelves and their commercials about youth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:44.940
nature were airing, all they had to do was&nbsp;
wait. During the first 8 years of the 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:50.100
bottled water would rocket in sales while&nbsp;
soda stagnated. During the next 8 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:56.460
bottled water continued to rocket while soda&nbsp;
declined leading us to where we are today.

00:11:56.460 --> 00:12:01.020
BOTTLED WATER DOMINATION:&nbsp;

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:06.480
In the end, the only reason that bottled water&nbsp;
is so popular today is because of a brilliant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:11.400
pivot from Pepsi and Coca Cola. They saw that&nbsp;
their core market was declining due to health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:16.920
concerns so they decided to create a new market&nbsp;
centered on health. The reality though is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:22.860
bottled water has nothing to do with health. In&nbsp;
fact, bottled water is less heavily regulated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:28.740
than tap water. The FDA does not require bottlers&nbsp;
to disclose water sources, treatment processes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:33.900
or even contaminant reports. Municipalities on&nbsp;
the other hand are required to send you this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:39.720
information on an annual basis, though of course&nbsp;
no one looks at these but atleast its there. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:44.040
means that there’s usually a lot more variation&nbsp;
regarding cleanliness when it comes to bottled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:50.100
water. One study found that tap water is pretty&nbsp;
consistent generally having between 0.2 to 2.7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:56.100
bacterial colonies per milliliter. Bottled water&nbsp;
on the other hand had anywhere between 0.01 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.100 --> 00:13:02.820
4900 colonies per milliliter. In fact, 6 of&nbsp;
the tested brands had 1500 to 4900 colonies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:08.520
per mililiter. So, the idea that bottled water&nbsp;
is healthier or cleaner is just a brilliantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:14.100
constructed facade and all the extra money you’re&nbsp;
spending on bottled water is just going to pockets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:20.400
of our favorite bottlers Coca Cola and Pepsi.&nbsp;
And that’s how America got brainwashed into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:26.040
drinking bottled water. Do you drink tap water&nbsp;
or bottled water? Comment that down below.&nbsp;&nbsp;

